1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,488
In southern India, the Nilgiri Hills are known as the Blue
Mountains.

2
00:00:04,488 --> 00:00:09,724
They are home to communities including the Toda, Kota, Kurumba,
Irula, Paniya and Kattunayaka,

3
00:00:09,724 --> 00:00:12,342
each with its own language and traditions.

4
00:00:12,342 --> 00:00:17,578
Badaga life is also rooted in these hills, with distinctive
food, close-knit villages, and

5
00:00:17,578 --> 00:00:19,822
a connection to farming and nature.

6
00:00:19,822 --> 00:00:22,814
Here, a language carries the sounds of home.

7
00:00:23,314 --> 00:00:24,954
Badaga is a Dravidian language.

8
00:00:24,954 --> 00:00:29,543
India's twenty eleven Census recorded one hundred thirty-three
thousand five hundred fifty Badaga mother-tongue

9
00:00:29,543 --> 00:00:29,871
speakers.

10
00:00:29,871 --> 00:00:33,805
This is a dated count in India, not a current worldwide
estimate.

11
00:00:33,805 --> 00:00:35,117
Siva Babu is Badaga.

12
00:00:35,117 --> 00:00:39,706
He moved abroad for growth and opportunity, while staying deeply
connected to his roots.

13
00:00:39,706 --> 00:00:44,296
He wants children growing up far from the hills to know the
language and

14
00:00:44,296 --> 00:00:46,263
culture that connect them to home.

15
00:00:46,763 --> 00:00:52,074
Badaga has a strong oral tradition, without a single broadly
standardised native writing system.

16
00:00:52,074 --> 00:00:57,005
People use Roman, Tamil and Kannada writing, and dedicated
scripts have been proposed.

17
00:00:57,005 --> 00:00:59,281
Spellings and family usage can differ.

18
00:00:59,281 --> 00:01:02,695
A written word cannot carry every sound or memory.

19
00:01:02,695 --> 00:01:08,005
Keeping the language present means making room for fluent
speakers, familiar voices, and everyday

20
00:01:08,005 --> 00:01:08,385
conversations.

21
00:01:08,385 --> 00:01:11,798
That is the challenge Siva wants to help address.

22
00:01:12,298 --> 00:01:16,015
Families leave the hills for education, work, and new
opportunities.

23
00:01:16,015 --> 00:01:21,217
For children overseas, daily life may happen mostly in English
or another local language.

24
00:01:21,217 --> 00:01:24,561
There may be few people nearby who speak Badaga.

25
00:01:24,561 --> 00:01:29,392
A child can love their grandparents and still struggle to
understand their stories.

26
00:01:29,392 --> 00:01:31,622
Siva wants to bridge that gap.

27
00:01:31,622 --> 00:01:36,825
Hatti Quest is his attempt to bring the language into small,
playful moments that

28
00:01:36,825 --> 00:01:37,939
families can share.

29
00:01:38,439 --> 00:01:42,441
The adventure begins on an illustrated trail inspired by the
hills.

30
00:01:42,441 --> 00:01:46,442
Every stop is open, and a lesson takes about three minutes.

31
00:01:46,442 --> 00:01:51,172
Children meet everyday words about family, conversation, nature,
counting, and life at home.

32
00:01:51,172 --> 00:01:55,901
They play gentle recognition games, collect badges, and revisit
words through spaced practice.

33
00:01:55,901 --> 00:01:59,538
There are fifty source-documented words and phrases in this
starter.

34
00:01:59,538 --> 00:02:04,631
The aim is a confident little beginning that can continue after
the screen is

35
00:02:04,631 --> 00:02:05,359
put away.

36
00:02:05,859 --> 00:02:09,350
Family corner lets a grown-up record a word in a familiar voice.

37
00:02:09,350 --> 00:02:13,422
A parent or grandparent can say it slowly, then the child can
hear that

38
00:02:13,422 --> 00:02:14,586
recording during a lesson.

39
00:02:14,586 --> 00:02:17,495
These short recordings stay in the browser on this device.

40
00:02:17,495 --> 00:02:19,822
They are never uploaded to the AI services.

41
00:02:19,822 --> 00:02:21,567
There is no automatic accent score.

42
00:02:21,567 --> 00:02:25,639
Families can keep their own way of saying a word alongside the
published source

43
00:02:25,639 --> 00:02:27,967
form, and download a recording as a keepsake.

44
00:02:28,467 --> 00:02:31,295
Story Studio brings the AI into the experience.

45
00:02:31,295 --> 00:02:36,244
A child chooses a moon mission, an animal adventure, a magical
sketchbook, or a

46
00:02:36,244 --> 00:02:36,951
little mystery.

47
00:02:36,951 --> 00:02:39,073
They choose a word topic too.

48
00:02:39,073 --> 00:02:44,022
The system retrieves relevant vocabulary and creates three short
English scenes around those word

49
00:02:44,022 --> 00:02:44,376
references.

50
00:02:44,376 --> 00:02:48,971
Python supplies the Badaga forms and the word games from the
documented collection.

51
00:02:48,971 --> 00:02:52,860
A small family activity carries the story back into everyday
life.

52
00:02:52,860 --> 00:02:56,396
An authored adventure remains available if live AI cannot
finish.

53
00:02:56,896 --> 00:02:59,682
The builder's dashboard makes the system visible.

54
00:02:59,682 --> 00:03:02,468
Nebius runs the embedding and story models.

55
00:03:02,468 --> 00:03:08,041
Pinecone searches a dedicated vocabulary namespace, and Python
combines those results with lexical search

56
00:03:08,041 --> 00:03:09,633
using reciprocal rank fusion.

57
00:03:09,633 --> 00:03:14,807
The model returns structured English fiction, then Python
validates the referenced word identifiers.

58
00:03:14,807 --> 00:03:19,584
The run ledger shows the sources, retrieval ranks, latency, and
provider-reported tokens.

59
00:03:19,584 --> 00:03:22,370
A cached adventure reports zero new tokens.

60
00:03:22,370 --> 00:03:27,146
Braintrust traces follow each stage, so a builder can inspect
what happened.

61
00:03:27,646 --> 00:03:31,037
The project measured retrieval on twenty-four curated English
queries.

62
00:03:31,037 --> 00:03:36,311
Hybrid search found the expected words within its top five
results in every case.

63
00:03:36,311 --> 00:03:39,324
The original lexical search achieved fifty-four percent recall.

64
00:03:39,324 --> 00:03:44,598
Eight generated adventures used valid source word references,
and all six grounding checks passed.

65
00:03:44,598 --> 00:03:49,495
These are small engineering checks, not proof of language
accuracy or learning effectiveness.

66
00:03:49,495 --> 00:03:51,755
Community speaker review is still pending.

67
00:03:51,755 --> 00:03:56,652
Fluent speakers and families can validate pronunciation,
preferred usage, and the learning experience.

68
00:03:57,152 --> 00:04:03,335
Prometheus scrapes the running Python application, and Grafana
displays the live metrics: service health,

69
00:04:03,335 --> 00:04:07,309
reported tokens, request modes, stage latency, and evaluation
scores.

70
00:04:07,309 --> 00:04:10,842
Braintrust holds the traces and the experiment record.

71
00:04:10,842 --> 00:04:13,933
Failures, fallbacks, and cache behaviour are inspectable.

72
00:04:13,933 --> 00:04:20,115
Siva used Codex to build this working Python prototype, its
tests, and the demo.

73
00:04:20,115 --> 00:04:26,297
The public GitHub repository includes the application,
evaluation dataset, and monitoring configuration, so other

74
00:04:26,297 --> 00:04:29,388
builders can reproduce and improve the work.

75
00:04:29,888 --> 00:04:35,070
The next step is community review: more family voices, corrected
word forms, and activities

76
00:04:35,070 --> 00:04:38,032
shaped by the people who use the language.

77
00:04:38,032 --> 00:04:43,214
The approach could support other languages with small digital
collections or strong oral traditions.

78
00:04:43,214 --> 00:04:48,026
Each needs its own sources, permissions, writing choices,
speaker review, and evaluation cases.

79
00:04:48,026 --> 00:04:51,727
For Siva, preserving Badaga means creating opportunities to
speak it.

80
00:04:51,727 --> 00:04:56,909
His hope is that the voices of elders become part of children's
everyday lives

81
00:04:56,909 --> 00:04:59,500
and their future, wherever they grow up.
